  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 AM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nAs the mid-latitude cyclone pushes off to the east this morning, the \nshowers will leave with it. Drier weather is expected through \nTuesday night as a high pressure system develops over the Great \nLakes. Temperatures warm back into the low 80s in western IL today, \nwith eastern IL in the upper 70s. Highs in the 80s and low in the \n60s will be the norm for the week. Temperatures look to approach 90 \ndegrees for highs by next Saturday and Sunday, with increasing \nhumidity. The LREF shows a 30-50% chance of apparent temperatures \nbeing greater than 96 degrees on Sunday afternoon across the entire \nforecast area. \n\nA more active pattern will return Wednesday as they upper flow gets \ndisturbed by a 500 mb short wave that will push a cold front into \nthe state. There is no shortage of CAPE on Wednesday, with MUCAPE \nvalues around 1500-2500 J/kg maximized over the western part of the \nstate. Bulk shear values are 30 knots or less for the same time \nperiod. This should support thunderstorm activity. The severe threat \nwith this wave of storms is low and machine learning models shows \nthe risk being pretty minimal as well. This front will possibly\nstall out over the area, bringing additional rounds of showers \nand thunderstorms into the weekend next week.",
